arguments.content:
# Detailed findings — gh-1107 PR review

🚨 BLOCKING: none.

⚠️ IMPORTANT: none.

💡 SUGGESTIONS: 4 (below).

---

## S1 — `postCheckRunStamp`'s `posted` flag is dead logic (`js/common/checkRunStamp.js:89`)

The function either throws (write/exec failure propagates) or returns `true`:

```js
function postCheckRunStamp(repoInfo, body, tempPath, io) {
    var posted = false;
    try {
        io.write({ path: tempPath, content: JSON.stringify(body) });
        io.exec({ command: stampCommand(repoInfo, tempPath) });
        posted = true;          // only reachable line before `return posted`
    } finally { ... }
    return posted;              // ⇒ always `true`
}
```

`posted = true` sits between the last throwing call and the `return`, so the
flag can never be `false` at the return. Simplify to no flag:

```js
function postCheckRunStamp(repoInfo, body, tempPath, io) {
    try {
        io.write({ path: tempPath, content: JSON.stringify(body) });
        io.exec({ command: stampCommand(repoInfo, tempPath) });
    } finally {
        try { io.del({ path: tempPath }); } catch (eDel) { /* best-effort cleanup */ }
    }
    return true;
}
```

Pure polish — behavior is identical either way.

---

## S2 — `truncateCheckRunSummary` can split a UTF-16 surrogate pair (`js/common/checkRunStamp.js:48`)

`s.slice(0, room)` slices by code units. If the cut lands between the two
halves of an astral character (emoji, some CJK), the body carries a lone
surrogate; `JSON.stringify` emits it as an unpaired escape (`\uD83D`), which
strict JSON decoders (GitHub's included) may reject or mojibake — turning the
E1 graceful truncation back into a failed stamp.

Requires a >65,535-char summary with an astral character exactly at the
boundary, so negligible likelihood today; a one-line guard closes it:

```js
var code = s.charCodeAt(room - 1);
if (code >= 0xD800 && code <= 0xDBFF) room -= 1; // don't split the pair
return s.slice(0, room) + SUMMARY_TRUNCATION_MARKER;
```

---

## S3 — jobs table is not markdown-escaped (`js/smAgent.js:7977`)

With the `"` → `'` mangling gone (correct — it was data corruption for the
shell era), a job name containing `]`, `[`, `|`, `(` or `)` now lands in the
check-run summary verbatim and can break the table row or the `[name](link)`
rendering — e.g. `Deploy [stage | eu]` produces a broken link cell. Purely
cosmetic (the Checks-API summary, not the shell — the injection vector is
gone). The AC1 byte-for-byte contract constrains the transport
(`buildCheckRunBody` must not alter bytes), not `stampLinkBlock`'s summary
construction, so escaping here would still comply:

```js
var safeName = String(j.name).replace(/([\[\]|])/g, '\\$1');
lines.push('| [' + safeName + '](' + link + ') | ' + verdict + ' |');
```

---

## S4 — missing trailing newline (`js/unit-tests/test_checkRunStamp.js:258`)

The new test file ends without a final newline (every other file in
`js/unit-tests/` has one). POSIX text-file convention; some diff/patch tooling
flags it.

